<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.jkrumm.opbackup</string>
  <!-- Hourly TRIGGER for the 1Password vault backup. The schedule is dumb on
       purpose; every decision about whether to actually run lives in
       opbackup-auto.sh (freshness stamp, attempt backoff, lock screen,
       1Password running, homelab reachable). 23 of 24 fires cost one stat(2).

       NOT RunAtLoad. That key fires only when the agent is LOADED — login or
       reboot — never on wake, and launchd.plist(5) explicitly says to avoid it
       ("speculative job launches have an adverse effect on system-boot and
       user-login scenarios"). The catch-up semantics wanted here belong to
       StartCalendarInterval, whose man page is unambiguous: "Unlike cron which
       skips job invocations when the computer is asleep, launchd will start the
       job the next time the computer wakes up. If multiple intervals transpire
       before the computer is woken, those events will be coalesced into one
       event upon wake from sleep." StartInterval does NOT do this — a fire
       missed while asleep is simply lost.

       A bare Minute is a wildcard on every other field, so this is every hour
       at :17 — not daily. A single daily fire that lands at a locked screen is
       skipped with the next chance 24h out, so a laptop shut at that one clock
       time drifts red while sitting open all afternoon. -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>__HOME__/SourceRoot/dotfiles/scripts/opbackup-auto.sh</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
  <dict>
    <key>Minute</key><integer>17</integer>
  </dict>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key><false/>
  <!-- launchd hands an agent /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin. `op`, `age`, `uv`
       (the backup's shebang is `uv run --script`) and `rsync` are all Homebrew,
       so the PATH has to be pinned here — same failure class as the
       collie LaunchAgent's missing `bun`. -->
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>PATH</key><string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
  </dict>
  <!-- ~/Library/Logs, never /tmp: macOS's periodic cleanup DELETES files there
       untouched for 3+ days and launchd opens this path once at spawn. Declared
       in scripts/log-rotate.sh. -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/opbackup.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>__HOME__/Library/Logs/opbackup.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
